%I A279405 #38 Aug 02 2026 16:41:44
%S A279405 0,0,0,2,2,4,4,8,7,12,10,18,16,25,20,32,28,42
%N A279405 Peaceable coexisting armies of queens on a torus: the maximum number m such that m white queens and m black queens can coexist on an n X n toroidal chessboard without attacking each other.
%C A279405 a(n) <= A250000(n).
%C A279405 a(n) is the maximum m such that A279406(n,m) >= m.
%H A279405 Katie Clinch, Matthew Drescher, Tony Huynh, and Abdallah Saffidine, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06974">Constructions, bounds, and algorithms for peaceable queens</a>, arXiv:2406.06974 [math.CO], 2024. See p. 1.
%H A279405 Jan Philipp Harries, <a href="https://github.com/jphme/math-problems/blob/e3f823ca5f382687eb52486fdf048476cefcb85b/peaceable-queens-even-torus/peace_even_torus.pdf">Peaceable queens on even tori: an exact parity formula</a>, 2026.
%H A279405 Andy Huchala, <a href="/A279405/a279405_4.py.txt">Python program</a>.
%F A279405 For q >= 1, a(2*q) = max min(r0*c1 + r1*c0, (q-r0)*(q-c0) + (q-r1)*(q-c1)), where the maximum is over integers r0, r1, c0, c1 in [0,q]. (For proof, see the Harries link.) - _Jan P. Harries_, Jul 27 2026
%e A279405 A solution for n=6:
%e A279405   ......
%e A279405   .W...W
%e A279405   ...B..
%e A279405   ..B.B.
%e A279405   ...B..
%e A279405   .W...W
%Y A279405 Cf. A250000, A279406.
%K A279405 nonn,hard,more,changed
%O A279405 1,4
%A A279405 _Andrei Zabolotskii_, Dec 11 2016
%E A279405 a(10)-a(12) from _Andy Huchala_, Mar 10 2024
%E A279405 a(13)-a(14) from _Jaideep Padhi_, Jun 20 2026
%E A279405 a(15)-a(18) from _Jan P. Harries_, Jul 27 2026
